UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 30, Lesson 2: Calendars and dates', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Calendars organise days into weeks and months. Find dates, count days between dates and order events on a calendar.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA calendar shows 1 April on a Saturday. What day is 8 April? How many days from 3 April to 10 April?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSaturday; 7 days.\n\nWATCH FOR\nForgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nUsing a real or printed month calendar, answer twelve questions (day of date, date of day, days between, order three events).\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Calendars organise days into weeks and months. Find dates, count days between dates and order events on a calendar.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Strategies","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-NSM-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Using a real or printed month calendar, answer twelve questions (day of date, date of day, days between, order three events). Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for calendar; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors.. Correction uses Saturday; 7 days.. Why: restores accurate calendar.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses calendar with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with calendar, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for calendar, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about calendar, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for calendar. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Saturday; 7 days","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"A calendar shows 1 April on a Saturday. What day is 8 April? How many days from 3 April to 10 April?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for calendar; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calendars organise days into weeks and months","Saturday; 7 days","Careful use of date and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with calendar?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named date and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply calendar to new material and name the date and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors","Name date and explain why it matters only, without linking it to calendar"],"question":"Which response best applies calendar with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on calendar?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught calendar method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Read 1 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 1 and 8 as interchangeable","Saturday; 7 days","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors"],"question":"Using the method for calendar, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a calendar problem with numbers such as 1 and 8, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model numbers 1 and 8 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors","Use the same calendar method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter"],"question":"Solve a fresh calendar problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Saturday; 7 days","Label units when comparing 1 with 8","Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors","Calendars organise days into weeks and months"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for calendar?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for calendar, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses calendar with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for calendar; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about calendar, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with calendar, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Forgetting that months have different lengths causes off-by-one date errors.. Correction uses Saturday; 7 days.. Why: restores accurate calendar.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w30-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise calendar.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use calendar with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify date and explain why it matters.","I can practise month independently on a new prompt.","I can notice order needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Calendars organise days into weeks and months.","options":["Calendars organise days into weeks and months","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about calendar with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIf 5 March is a Tuesday, then 12 March is also Tuesday (add 7 days). There are 31 days in March.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA calendar shows 1 April on a Saturday. What day is 8 April? How many days from 3 April to 10 April?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSaturday; 7 days.","explain":"Calendars organise days into weeks and months. Find dates, count days between dates and order events on a calendar.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by